From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:31:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09072/11362] Kaggle v15 preview A/B: concurrency at real
 prompt size is slower than sequential

First data from the free preview channel, 12 arms in 608s on the real scored card,
no submission slot consumed.

FINDING 1. At induce-realistic prompt size concurrency COSTS more than it saves:
k=1 9.34s, k=4 58.71s, k=8 144.79s, with per-stream throughput falling 27.4 ->
14.1 tok/s. Four sequential requests would take about 37.4s against 58.71s
concurrent, so concurrency is ~1.6x SLOWER. At small prompts it inverts (k=4 small
is 190 tok/s aggregate against 7.37 at k=1), so batching helps for short prompts
and hurts for induce-sized ones -- and the scored path uses induce-sized prompts.

This settles exp6251's open caveat with a measurement. That artifact reported
mean_batching_efficiency 3.604 and I flagged that it measures OVERLAP, not
speed-up, because the sequential arm never ran. Measured now, the truth is worse
than "no speed-up": best-of-N over concurrent requests costs more wall-clock than
running them sequentially. Future best-of-N designs should treat concurrency as a
cost, not a discount.

FINDING 2. `--parallel 8` breaks the induce prompt outright: HTTP 400, "request
(21880 tokens) exceeds the available context size (13312 tokens)". With -c 106496
and --parallel 8 each slot gets 13312 tokens and a real induce prompt does not fit.
This confirms on the scored card what _default_induce_n_ctx's docstring warned in
prose. The CARNOT_ARC_LLAMA_SERVER_SLOTS knob is safe as shipped because it only
changes the n_ctx arithmetic and never passes --parallel; anyone wiring --parallel
to it must raise -c proportionally.

FINDING 3, a real defect. The harness reported
constants=fallback:ImportError("cannot import name '_llama_server_slots'"), so the
Kaggle carnot-agent-code dataset does not contain the 2026-08-11 knob work and the
scored path runs older code than this repo. Every arm therefore measured the
hardware, not the shipped configuration -- which the harness announced in a loud
CONFIG WARNING rather than letting it pass silently, so that design earned its
place on first contact. Re-uploading the dataset is an external publishing action
and stays operator-gated; it is the cheapest thing that would make the scored run
match this repo.

